---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { ORCHARDS } from '../lib/orchards'
import { SOURCES } from '../lib/sources'

const counts = new Map<string, number>()
for (const o of ORCHARDS) {
  counts.set(o.import_source, (counts.get(o.import_source) ?? 0) + 1)
}

const upick = ORCHARDS.filter((o) => o.tags.includes('pick_your_own')).length
const cider = ORCHARDS.filter((o) => o.tags.includes('craft_cider')).length
const sites = ORCHARDS.filter((o) => o.website).length

const byState = new Map<string, number>()
for (const o of ORCHARDS) {
  const key = o.state ?? '?'
  byState.set(key, (byState.get(key) ?? 0) + 1)
}
const states = [...byState.entries()].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
const fromOsm = ORCHARDS.filter((o) => o.import_source === 'osm').length
---
<Base
  title="About | Orchard Map"
  description="Where the orchard data comes from, what is missing from it, and why the missing parts are shown as missing."
>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    {ORCHARDS.length} apple orchards, cider mills and farm markets within about two
    and a half hours of New York City — {upick} of them pick-your-own, {cider} making
    hard cider or spirits.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the listings come from</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://www.applesfromny.com/" rel="noreferrer nofollow">New York Apple
    Association</a>'s member directory. It carries names, addresses, coordinates,
    phone numbers, websites and categories, which is more than any other single
    source has.
  </p>
  <p>
    OpenStreetMap was the obvious first choice and turned out not to work. Across
    this whole region, <code>self_harvesting=yes</code> — the OSM tag that means
    pick-your-own — appears on <strong>exactly one</strong> feature, and only 82
    farm shops carry a website at all. It is a good source of orchard
    <em>polygons</em> and a poor source of orchard <em>facts</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The association states no licence for its directory. That is ambiguity rather
    than permission, so every row here records where it came from and when, and
    withdrawing the source would be one filter rather than an archaeology project.
  </p>

  <h2>Coverage is uneven, and here is exactly how</h2>
  <table class="facts">
    <tbody>
      {states.map(([state, n]) => (
        <tr>
          <th>{state}</th>
          <td>
            {n} {n === 1 ? 'listing' : 'listings'}
            {state !== 'NY' && <span class="unknown"> — from OpenStreetMap only</span>}
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    New York, Connecticut and Pennsylvania each have a state-level source. New York's
    is the richest — its association publishes categories, so those listings arrive
    knowing whether a farm does pick-your-own. Connecticut's and Pennsylvania's are
    rosters: names, addresses, phones and websites, with few attributes. Those get
    filled in by reading the farms' own sites.
  </p>
  <p>
    Massachusetts is still {fromOsm} OpenStreetMap leftovers — pins with names and
    little else. That is a gap in <em>our data</em>, not a shortage of orchards;
    please do not read a thin state as a state without farms.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>New Jersey is the worst of it.</strong> The state's own directory,
    findjerseyfresh.com, serves <code>Disallow: /</code> to every crawler — the whole
    site. That is a clear answer and it is respected; nothing here touches it. So New
    Jersey depends on OpenStreetMap and on people adding farms themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Connecticut</strong> comes from the
    <a href="https://ctapples.org/find-a-farm/" rel="noreferrer nofollow">Connecticut Apple
    Marketing Board</a>, and <strong>Pennsylvania</strong> from
    <a href="https://papreferred.com/search" rel="noreferrer nofollow">PA Preferred</a>,
    the state Department of Agriculture's branding programme. Pennsylvania is thinner
    than it looks it should be for a reason: Adams County, the state's apple capital,
    is about two hundred miles from New York City and falls outside the day-trip
    radius entirely. What is here is the north-east — the Lehigh Valley, Bucks County
    and the Pocono foothills.
  </p>

  <h2>Greenmarkets are not on the map</h2>
  <p>
    The directory also lists 51 New York City greenmarket stalls — a folding table on
    a pavement on 175th Street. That is a fact about the grower who drives down, not
    about the corner, and somebody looking for somewhere to spend Saturday does not
    want them in the results. Farms that sell at greenmarkets <em>and</em> grow
    apples, like Fishkill Farms, are here.
  </p>

  <h2>What is missing, and why it says so</h2>
  <p>
    Opening hours, whether picking is open, prices, varieties, dogs, restrooms,
    hayrides, cider donuts — none of it is checked yet. {sites} of these farms have a
    website, and a scraper that reads them is the next piece of work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Until then those fields are shown as unchecked rather than as "no". The
    distinction matters more here than it looks: a wrong "open" costs somebody a
    two-hour drive with children in the car, and the only thing worse than a map
    that does not know is a map that pretends to.
  </p>

  <h2>Licences</h2>
  <p>
    Every listing records where it came from and under what terms, so withdrawing a
    source is one filter rather than an archaeology project.
  </p>
  <p>
    A few rows carry a field from a second source — an OpenStreetMap listing whose
    address comes from the state directory, say. Those fields are recorded
    individually and credited individually on the farm's own page, rather than
    letting the row's label take credit for work another source did. Withdrawing a
    source therefore means dropping its rows <em>and</em> clearing that short list
    of fields, which is still one query rather than an archaeology project.
  </p>
  {/* The same list the orchard pages credit from, so this cannot drift out of
      agreement with what a visitor reads on the farm itself. */}
  <ul>
    {SOURCES.map((s) => (
      <li>
        <strong>{s.name}</strong> — {s.licenceUrl
          ? <a href={s.licenceUrl} rel="noreferrer">{s.licence}</a>
          : s.licence}{s.note ? `. ${s.note}` : '.'}
        {' '}<span class="muted">({counts.get(s.key) ?? 0} listings)</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Driving times</h2>
  <p>
    When you say where you are travelling from, the drive times come from
    <a href="https://routing.openstreetmap.de/about.html" rel="noreferrer">OSRM</a>,
    the demo server sponsored by FOSSGIS, running on OpenStreetMap data. One
    request per origin, for every orchard at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>There is no traffic in those numbers.</strong> They are free-flow
    road times, and a Saturday morning in late September is exactly when that
    is most wrong. Treat them as a way to sort the list, not as an arrival
    time — which is why every one of them says "without traffic" next to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/minormending/orchard-map" rel="noreferrer">github.com/minormending/orchard-map</a>.
    Corrections are welcome, and accounts for submitting them are coming.
  </p>
</Base>
